# Terraform
**/.terraform/*
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terraformrc
terraform.rc

# Secret-bearing variable files (non-secret terraform.tfvars is committed)
*.secret.tfvars
secrets.auto.tfvars

# Local shell env (e.g. direnv's .envrc holding HCLOUD_TOKEN — Read Only
# only, per AGENTS.md; never the Read & Write token)
.envrc
.env

# OS/editor cruft
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Working trees. AGENTS.md requires every change to take one — at
# `.worktrees/<name>` generally, or `.claude/worktrees/<name>` under the Claude
# Code binding — and each is a full copy of the repository living inside it.
# Untracked and unignored, they showed up in `git status` and stood one
# `git add -A` from the main working tree away from being committed wholesale.
#
# This does NOT keep them out of the CI-configuration suite's walks: a
# filesystem walk does not consult git. That is `walked_files()`'s prune list,
# and `terraform_lockfile_directories()` explains why the two are separate
# fixes. Only `.claude/worktrees/` is ignored, not `.claude/`, whose
# `commands/` and `skills/` are committed.
.worktrees/
.claude/worktrees/

# The per-working-tree ANSIBLE_HOME that ansible/scripts/run-molecule points
# Molecule at, holding this tree's own ephemeral directories. Machine state, not
# repository content — and it must not be shared, which is the whole point.
.molecule-home/

# Ansible: Galaxy-installed role content, pinned by exact version in that
# file, not committed itself. Fetched via:
#   ansible-galaxy role install -r ansible/requirements.yml -p ansible/roles
ansible/roles/geerlingguy.docker/

# Python bytecode from the CI-configuration test suite (.github/tests/).
__pycache__/

# npm's install tree for .github/package.json, which pins the OpenSpec CLI that
# pr-validation.yml validates the specification record with. The manifest and
# its lockfile ARE committed -- they are the pin; what is installed from them
# is not. `npm ci` recreates this from the lockfile exactly.
node_modules/

# Private keys. None is ever committed, and nothing else here says so.
#
# The hazard is specific rather than general: docs/bootstrap-a-new-host.md
# stage 0.3 generates passphrase-less keypairs, stage 4.2 runs `git add -A`
# from this directory, and those two steps are most of a working day apart.
# docs/onboard-an-application.md generates one more, per application per
# environment, on its own occasion. gitleaks catches the content at commit time
# -- but it is a pre-commit hook, and stage 0 runs before `pre-commit install`
# necessarily has. This is the layer that does not depend on having installed
# anything.
#
# Conventional names, anywhere in the tree:
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519

# And this repository's own scheme, which produces names with NO extension --
# <company>-ops, <company>-root, <company>-platform-<environment>,
# <company>-ansible-ci-<stack>, <company>-<app>-<environment> -- so no suffix
# pattern reaches them. Both documents above write each into ~/.ssh/; these
# catch the relative-path slip that writes one here instead.
#
# ANCHORED TO THE ROOT with a leading slash, deliberately. Unanchored, `*-ops`
# would silently swallow a directory called `commerce-ops` added later, which
# is the same class of accident this block exists to prevent. The slip lands in
# the directory the command is run from, and that is this one.
#
# TWO PATTERNS PER PURPOSE WOULD GO STALE AGAIN, which is what happened: the
# scheme renamed <company>-<app>-deploy to <company>-<app>-<environment> and
# gave the platform key an environment suffix, and no pattern followed. So the
# two purposes whose names END in an environment are covered by that suffix
# rather than by a pattern each -- one per environment, not one per purpose --
# and a third application needs no edit here, nor a fourth key purpose whose
# name also ends in an environment. One that does not -- a <company>-backup --
# is matched by nothing and fails the check below until a pattern is added,
# which is the design rather than a hole in it. The set of environments is this
# repository's own and small: the files in ansible/inventory/group_vars/ other
# than all.yml.
#
# What that costs is a root-level path ending in `-staging` or `-production`,
# which would be ignored silently. Nothing here is named that way -- a stack
# directory lives under terraform/stacks/ -- and
# .github/tests/test_the_bootstrap_documents_static_conventions.py fails the
# build if a DIRECTORY at this root ever matches a pattern below. A root-level
# file that matches is not reported, and must not be: that is the slip these
# patterns exist to catch. The same module asserts the other direction: that
# every `ssh-keygen -f` target either runbook prints is matched here. That
# check, rather than a reader, is what keeps this block in step with the two
# documents that generate keys.
/*-ops
/*-root
/*-ansible-ci-*
/*-staging
/*-production

# Retired spellings, kept rather than removed: each costs nothing, and a
# workstation still holding a key generated before the rename is exactly the
# case this block exists for. `/*-platform` is the un-suffixed platform key and
# `/*-deploy` the <company>-<app>-deploy application key; no document generates
# either now.
/*-platform
/*-deploy

# Public halves are not secret, and one being hidden would be its own defect.
!*.pub
